      <section class="section section-alt">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="portfolio-grid case-study-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Project Overview</span>
              <h2>What the system does and why teams depended on it</h2>
              <p>Nexlance is a marketplace platform built for freelancers and businesses buying structured digital services. The product was designed to create a trustworthy service marketplace instead of a loose collection of listings and off-platform coordination.</p>
              <p>From an engineering perspective, the system had to support marketplace discovery, transaction workflows, messaging, and payment flows for many listings and concurrent buyers. That made it a production platform with real operational demands rather than a portfolio demo.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel">
              <span class="section-kicker">Business Challenge</span>
              <h2>What had to improve for the product to scale cleanly</h2>
              <p>The core business challenge was delivering a product that could serve marketplace software and gig economy platforms use cases while still remaining easy for operators to trust and use every day.</p>
              <p>The client needed stronger order clarity, seller credibility, and transaction reliability so the marketplace could scale without a support-heavy operating model.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading compact-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Constraints &amp; Complexity</span>
              <h3>Engineering realities that shaped implementation choices</h3>
            </div>
            <p>The delivery had to solve product problems, operational risk, and scaling concerns at the same time.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="expertise-grid project-complexity-grid">
            
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Two-Sided UX</h3>
              <p>The product had to serve buyers and freelancers with different goals inside one interface.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Order State Clarity</h3>
              <p>Transactions needed unambiguous progress tracking for both sides of the marketplace.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Trust & Reputation</h3>
              <p>Ratings, profiles, and service presentation directly affected conversion.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="feature-card">
              <i class="bi bi-shield-check"></i>
              <h3>Payment Safety</h3>
              <p>Marketplace payment workflows needed dependable handling and visibility.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section section-accent">
        <div class="container" data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-delay="100">
          <div class="section-heading">
            <div>
              <span class="section-kicker">Solution Architecture</span>
              <h2>System design, integrations, and delivery approach</h2>
            </div>
            <p>The implementation emphasized maintainability, production-readiness, and cleaner operational workflows.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="case-study-hero architecture-layout">
            <article class="content-panel">
              <h3>Implementation approach</h3>
              <p>The solution used Laravel, Blade, AWS S3, Redis, PayPal, Stripe, MySQL across a modular application architecture. Core workflows were separated into clearer domain boundaries so business logic, integrations, and UI concerns did not become tightly coupled.</p>
              <p>That approach made the system easier to scale, easier to monitor, and easier to extend as new requirements appeared. It also created better release confidence for a product expected to handle real customer traffic and operator activity.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="content-panel architecture-diagram-panel">
              <span class="eyebrow">Architecture Diagram</span>
              <div class="architecture-diagram" aria-label="System architecture">
                <div class="arch-node">Marketplace UI</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">Domain Services</div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-row">
                  <div class="arch-node small">Payments</div>
                  <div class="arch-node small">Operator Tools</div>
                </div>
                <div class="arch-arrow bi bi-arrow-down"></div>
                <div class="arch-node">MySQL + Cache</div>
              </div>
            </article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

